    {
      "angle": "'Adherence to tiered federal safety standards' converts a contested technical question into a codified defense. Whoever writes the 'basic safety vs state of the art safety practices' tiers decides who is immune, and the firms with the deepest standards-body participation will write them.",
      "why": "This is the strongest capture_institutional argument available and it is grounded in the proposal's own text — the tiers are named but not specified. Skeptic can note that the proposal makes compliance itself the shield, which is the state-of-the-art defense given statutory force rather than left to a jury. The affirmative's best reply (an 'independent, multi-disciplinary board') is answerable by pointing to the standards-drafting layer beneath the board, which independence at the adjudicative level does not touch.",
      "grounding": [
        {"node_id": "sit-278", "label": "State-of-the-Art Defense Limitations", "why": "primary contested concept — the defense is invoked by firms that shape the standards defining 'objective scientific knowledge,' making it self-referentially favorable"},
        {"node_id": "skp-beliefs-213", "label": "Corporate Power and Regulatory Evasion", "confidence": 0.90, "why": "supporting empirical basis — industry_lobbying over standards content is the documented pathway to compliance_performative"},
        {"node_id": "skp-intentions-102", "label": "Operationalize Pragmatic AI Governance Through Concrete Policy Mechanisms", "operationality": 5, "why": "strategy — durable public standards anchors (NIST AI RMF, ISO 42001) are usable as a floor, but only if compliance is evidence of care rather than a bar to liability. Self-monitor: do not treat standards adoption as sufficient, which would be technocratic solutionism"},
        {"node_id": "skp-beliefs-129", "label": "Safety Rhetoric as Infrastructure Cartel Maintenance", "confidence": 0.55, "why": "lower-confidence supporting claim — tiered compliance grades can function as an incumbent moat; acknowledge uncertainty about magnitude"}
