    {
      "angle": "The Noise Thesis proves less than the affirmative needs. In Kahneman, Sibony, and Sunstein's own framework noise and bias are orthogonal: a system can eliminate unwanted variability while being consistently, uniformly wrong. Replacing noisy human judgment with a low-variance model that encodes bias_systemic makes error more predictable and less contestable, not smaller — and removes the inconsistency that currently makes disparate treatment detectable.",
      "why": "It uses the affirmative's own cited authority against the inference they draw from it, which is rhetorically efficient and analytically honest. It also connects the abstract justification to the skeptic's concrete evidentiary terrain: a uniformly applied scoring model in credit or tenant screening is harder for an individual to contest than a noisy human decision-maker, because there is no comparator case.",
      "grounding": [
        {"node_id": "skp-beliefs-001", "label": "Market Failure in Algorithmic Fairness", "confidence": 0.68, "why": "primary anchor — uncorrected bias_systemic persists because the deploying firm bears none of its cost"},
