    },
    {
      "angle": "The 'liability chill' premise is unevidenced, and the 'power disparity between tech giants and individuals' premise is self-refuting as a warrant for immunity. The proposal cites asymmetry_power between firms and individuals and then proposes to resolve it by removing the individual's only leverage.",
      "why": "The affirmative asserts throttled deployment without a case, at a moment of record deployment across healthcare and social media — the two domains the proposal names. Skeptic should place the burden of production on the proponent rather than trying to prove a negative. The judo on asymmetry_power is the single most quotable move available: the diagnosis is correct, the prescription inverts it.",
      "grounding": [
        {"node_id": "skp-beliefs-029", "label": "Victims of AI Harm Cannot Access the Technical Knowledge Needed to Prove Causation", "confidence": 0.65, "why": "primary anchor — the existing asymmetry_power already runs against claimants, so the litigation threat is far weaker than 'unlimited liability' implies"},
        {"node_id": "skp-desires-087", "label": "Anchoring AI accountability in human decisions and material power relations rather than system mental states", "priority": 4, "why": "normative commitment — material power relations are the correct analytic frame, and immunity shifts them further toward developers"},
