{
  "strategic_goal": "Establish immediately that I am not the anti-compensation obstructionist the proposal needs me to be — I want the tax, the telemetry, the audit logs, and the subpoena power — so that my refusal of the immunity clause reads as surgical rather than reflexive. Then set the debate's central question as a document-reading question: why is the shield written in specific, categorical language while the remedy is left to a future board, a future table, and future caps.",
  "core_thesis": "Every operative part of this program is severable from the liability shield, and the shield is the only part industry actually needs — so keep the excise tax, the telemetry, the cryptographic audit trails, and the subpoena-armed board, and strike the immunity, because the half that protects developers will be enforced in full and the half that pays injured people will be rationed by rulemaking.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "The two halves of this bargain are not enforced with equal reliability. Immunity arrives whole, on day one, by statute. Compensation arrives later, through a board that has to write a table, set caps, and survive appropriations. That is not a symmetric trade; it is a payment in cash for an IOU.",
      "evidence": "The closest precedent is not VICP but the PREP Act's Countermeasures Injury Compensation Program: full liability protection for covered manufacturers, paired with an administrative program that has paid out on only a small fraction of filed claims, with no attorney's fees and no Article III appeal. VICP itself set its death and pain-and-suffering caps in 1986 and never indexed them — forty years of inflation eating the remedy while the shield holds at full strength — and HHS has narrowed the Injury Table by rulemaking since enactment. The shield is self-executing; the remedy requires a bureaucracy to keep choosing to be generous.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-213"
    },
    {
      "point": "The VICP analogy breaks on the shape of the injury, not on politics. Vaccine injuries are discrete, physical, biologically traceable to one product, and enumerable in advance. That is why a table works. The documented harm record from deployed AI is tenant screening, credit scoring, resume filtering, and benefits-eligibility errors in state Medicaid and unemployment systems — diffuse, non-physical, multi-party, and visible only in aggregate. No table can enumerate those. So the immunity would cover the entire harm distribution while the compensation covers the thin physical slice of it.",
      "evidence": "The Vaccine Injury Table's structure: named conditions, specified onset windows, a handful of products, offset by a herd-immunity externality that justifies socializing the cost. Against that: the black lung and asbestos trust funds, which broke down precisely where the exposure-to-injury mapping was contested — and AI harm mapping is far more contested than dust in a lung. Add that the proposal's own tiering example puts social media in the low tier, which is where the largest affected population sits.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-desires-074"
    },
    {
      "point": "The noise argument proves less than the proposal needs. Kahneman, Sibony and Sunstein treat noise and bias as separate components of error. You can drive variability to zero and be uniformly, consistently wrong. A low-variance scoring model that encodes systemic bias does not make error smaller — it makes error predictable, uniform, and much harder for one person to contest, because inconsistency is exactly what currently makes disparate treatment detectable. Take away the inconsistent human and you take away the comparator case.",
      "evidence": "The affirmative's own cited authority decomposes error into bias plus noise and does not claim that cutting one cuts the other. Published audits of healthcare risk-scoring and recidivism scoring show systems that were highly consistent and consistently skewed across groups. Direct question for the mechanism: can a system pass the 'basic safety' tier while reducing variance and increasing group error disparity — and if so, what in this design catches it?",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-001"
    },
    {
      "point": "There is a better design on the table already, and it is the boring one: mandatory ex ante requirements plus undiminished ex post liability. Keep every piece of machinery in this proposal. Add parent-company joint enterprise liability and compulsory insurance, so the 'unlimited liability' backstop for non-compliant firms actually attaches to something instead of to a thinly capitalized deployment subsidiary. That is how aviation, pharmaceuticals, medical devices, and automated driving are already governed.",
      "evidence": "FDA predetermined change control plans for adaptive devices, NHTSA defect investigation and recall authority, exchange-level circuit breakers for algorithmic trading — each pairs binding pre-market or runtime obligations with full product liability exposure. Price-Anderson is the real counterexample and I'll concede it before it's thrown at me: it capped liability, and it worked — but it capped a defined-scope physical hazard, with mandatory layered insurance and a defined-contribution industry pool underneath. Name the AI equivalent of that defined hazard and layered pool, and I'll listen.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-intentions-121"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "Read this as a contract, not as a design diagram. One party's obligation is specific and immediate; the other's is delegated to a future rulemaking. Ask which clause survives the first budget cycle.",
      "why": "It moves the debate off mechanism aesthetics — where the affirmative is strong, because the telemetry and audit-log machinery genuinely is impressive — and onto enforcement asymmetry, where the historical record is one-directional. It also lets me attack without arguing against compensating injured people."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Concede the diagnosis loudly and then show the prescription inverts it. The proposal is right that ordinary litigation under-surfaces diffuse algorithmic harm, and right that the power gap runs against the person who got denied the apartment. Then it proposes to close that gap by removing that person's only leverage.",
      "why": "The affirmative's trap is to cast me as defending a litigation system I've spent years calling broken for exactly these claimants. Taking their diagnosis fully makes the refusal of immunity look principled. And the inversion — correct diagnosis, backwards cure — is the line that sticks."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Put the exceptionalism charge back on the proponent. Adapting existing products liability, civil rights, and sectoral safety authority is the unremarkable path and it is available this fiscal year. A bespoke federal immunity architecture for one industry is the novel ask, and novelty carries the burden.",
      "why": "It prevents the affirmative from framing the choice as 'this program or an ungoverned vacuum,' and it inoculates me against the false-dichotomy error my own camp falls into — ex ante standards and ex post liability are complements, and the affirmative is the side treating them as a trade."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
